    "title": "In 2008, Leonardo DiCaprio moved into a $3.67 million solar-powered tower in Manhattan, and its green features were years ahead of their time | Hindustan Times",
    "text": "A neighborhood with green rules\nThe story begins with the neighborhood itself, not the tower. Battery Park City sits on 92 acres of landfill in the southwestern corner of Manhattan. A 2004 paper, ‘Charging the Battery: Energy Efficiency in Battery Park City,’ explains what happened next. In the late 1990s, the Battery Park City Authority decided on an interesting move. All new construction on its remaining land had to follow the green guidelines. A New York State tax credit for green buildings also provided a boost for developers, the authors note. So, the area used rules and rewards to encourage people to build greener homes.",
